ether with the  u.s president joe biden a vaccine  partnership eu us vaccine partnership  to beat the global pandemic  and we've set ourselves the goal to have  70 percent of the global population  vaccinated by next year  the european union will donate more than  500 million doses within the next month  and also the united states will do their  share but of course other countries have  to step up and to contribute to  and therefore i'm working very closely  now with the g20 presidency that is  italy mario draghi of italy and the  american president that this weekend at  the g20 summit we rally  the leaders around the goal to have 70  percent of the world's population  vaccinated by next year and thus to step  up the donations and to step up  the the manufacturing of life-saving  vaccines for the whole world  this is the focus at the moment being  but of course at the same same time we  have to prepare ourselves for future  health threats  a great european jack de la once said  in the face of a crisis  you need both  a fireman  as well as an architect  so um the fire fighter i'd say other  vaccines  but let's talk for a moment about the  two pillars  of the future architecture  the lessons we've drawn out of this  crisis first of all we have created a  new health emergency preparedness and  response authority long name  in short hera  hera's task is to better prepare for  health emergencies  to pro to detect them more earlier  and promptly  and to respond collectively  all three things we didn't have when  coveted started we were not prepared we  had no clue how to to detect it early  enough and we had no joint cooperation  in place we all had to learn that  so the scope of hero will reach from  collective procurement for example of  protective equipment medicines and  vaccines  to coordinating for example the rules  for testing and quarantine and all you  can think of what is necessary if you're  faced with such a cross-border health  threat  we will also strengthen the european  pharmaceutical sector and the health  ecosystem  in which belgium is such a prominent  actor  and this brings me to the second point  here we have the innovation and the  scientific capacity here in belgium and  in the european union  we have the private sector knowledge if  i look at do you hear assembled here in  this room i mean there's a huge amount  of knowledge and scientific and  innovation capacity  and we have competent national  authorities  so what we need now  is to bring all these elements together  and to underpin them with massive  investment funding  let me give you some examples take the  european innovation council that for  example supports now the highly  innovative smes  in the area of infectious diseases  or just recently  this european innovation council  announced financial support  for belgium biotech company that  develops tools for precision microbiome  profiling  and of course then there is our huge  recovery fund we call it next generation  eu 800 billion euros in today's today's  prices  and here the member states are using  these funds  indeed to make them available  to the research community to build  resilience healthcare capacities and to  invest in innovation  belgium  for example prime minister was very  happy to see is investing in a biotech  school  are you investing  into research and development supporting  the production of medical  radio isotopes  so all this  taking  the research capacities and  joining them with the necessary funding  bringing it all together all this helps  us  to prepare better for future health  threats  and i think there is no better return on  investment than that  distinguished guests this is our  european way to do things  while of course repairing the damage of  the crisis  we are investing in a better future  and it is great to see that with this  biopharma platform you are creating  today  you are also going in absolutely the  same direction  so i count on belgium to continue to  